#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "973cf948-eeb3-5c46-b0ac-ee0307a8043a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06dd37ec-93a9-5b21-89e4-561d7cf50bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256841} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8e4a81-ce37-5a0a-bf71-1d9989cbe036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256881} {:answer/id #uuid "297e7d94-4a80-565f-88bd-e5215df2ba8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257151} {:answer/id #uuid "4e711455-77c9-5616-92bb-d797cf1c8968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257454} {:answer/id #uuid "da8ca18d-520e-5091-a63b-d25c9509aa0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e711455-77c9-5616-92bb-d797cf1c8968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257454} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b3878a-36da-5c94-8d4e-f1633afbca83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c8bb8d5-c684-5d53-8ebb-fc2e85b6f1e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-123355" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fbf4c3074641b18d12dc7372378550f4c37e0b9b81843c2e57629c891a59263.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "973cf948-eeb3-5c46-b0ac-ee0307a8043a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17652adb-f0bd-5e06-b55e-fb60b41d8171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447094} {:answer/id #uuid "392cdecf-382d-5eaf-8512-13e93f090b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447104} {:answer/id #uuid "bb292c1a-79be-51a1-91b7-2783d7d709cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447133} {:answer/id #uuid "e84048f5-bc2b-5cb4-a79e-ebf71a21e3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447139} {:answer/id #uuid "fdef13fa-c9e2-5073-8dc0-04da2e76ab8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "392cdecf-382d-5eaf-8512-13e93f090b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447104} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cca78f2-2d1d-5264-9180-c7409ad32bd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "586f6a61-ad62-56e5-9a2c-611ea0a2819c" :question/math-academy-id "q-123379" :question/problem "![](images/19/19725cfd8068973c899fcb4f54abcebbbfcd24bd68aec510b9d41cee2c2cd02a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{20-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "973cf948-eeb3-5c46-b0ac-ee0307a8043a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e65c36-be44-5f01-9c76-e2d474ae0429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{16+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482823} {:answer/id #uuid "416c60e9-e558-531b-9580-88325c39f159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{18-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482921} {:answer/id #uuid "d1393249-3bf9-5edc-8048-09a57dbf1249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{8-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483160} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fcdafd-bf7d-5de0-8c88-50a987f3891f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} {:answer/id #uuid "e65466c4-bccb-5f49-930c-7f3a5aefd1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{10-26\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1fcdafd-bf7d-5de0-8c88-50a987f3891f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b07591-e098-5b5d-80e3-d30542d3cd68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49e7f17d-b251-56fb-a73b-9aa663a5feb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-123410" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef5fe44009ea42457243052cff584a216ee94636d8e3c16ff9446e85b8eca551.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +25{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "973cf948-eeb3-5c46-b0ac-ee0307a8043a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfa492a8-e07a-522a-bf45-34af18c7a267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{2} θ\\tan^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168598} {:answer/id #uuid "1813a2d0-d5bd-52b4-99b5-efcb1d2a7640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168599} {:answer/id #uuid "2aba48aa-c769-5c6a-9e07-f0c67be25a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + 9\\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168600} {:answer/id #uuid "55f60188-6938-5f92-aae8-dedef3328cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{9 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168601} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3a3ab0-69fc-59cc-bc73-08e310482454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\sec^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168602}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d4363fdf-f405-59b8-b8f3-f8bb7ba8806f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168597} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d61aec87-e699-59eb-980b-a6a6cf0bb0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405024} {:answer/id #uuid "94ab8694-5f19-5ea3-8d98-092aec26cb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405025} {:answer/id #uuid "a57cacb1-b76d-579c-8c67-b4527f5232ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{9+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405026} {:answer/id #uuid "1eabb9a5-ecc0-5827-9b8a-fa684cf58678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405027} {:answer/id #uuid "9f675dbc-4798-5a7f-b170-06e1c7c60f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d61aec87-e699-59eb-980b-a6a6cf0bb0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405024} :answer-field/id #uuid "9492d74f-ea7c-56c6-a9a7-679ef961d276" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f58568c3-ce47-5162-ad81-da8c754e43d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-123439" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is obtained by using the pointwise polar arc-length integrand for ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\pi /2<\\theta<\\pi /2$? Interpret the endpoint integrals as improper integrals." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({sec}^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta +9{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese endpoint integrals are improper and diverge to $+\\infty.$ The requested expression is determined by the pointwise arc-length integrand on $-\\pi/2<\\theta<\\pi/2.$"}]}
